      "text": "The arithmetic progression $\\{2, 5, 8, 11, \\ldots\\}$ can be represented as \\(2 + 3n\\), where \\(n\\) is a non-negative integer. This means the \\(n\\)-th term can be written as:\n\\[ a_n = 2 + 3n \\]\n\nThe arithmetic progression $\\{3, 10, 17, 24, \\ldots \\}$ can be represented as \\(7 + 7k\\), where \\(k\\) is a non-negative integer. This means the \\(k\\)-th term can be written as:\n\\[ b_k = 7 + 7k \\]\n\nWe need to find the common terms of these two arithmetic progressions, which are given by the equation:\n\\[ 2 + 3n = 7 + 7k \\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[ 3n - 7k = 5 \\]\nThis is a linear Diophantine equation. To solve it, we need to find particular solutions for \\(n\\) and \\(k\\). One way to find a particular solution is by trying small values for \\(n\\) and solving for \\(k\\).\n\nLet's try \\(n = 2\\):\n\\[ 3(2) - 7k = 5 \\]\n\\[ 6 - 7k = 5 \\]\n\\[ 6 - 5 = 7k \\]\n\\[ 1 = 7k \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{1}{7} \\]\nSince \\(k\\) is not an integer, \\(n = 2\\) is not a solution.\n\nLet's try \\(n = 4\\):\n\\[ 3(4) - 7k = 5 \\]\n\\[ 12 - 7k = 5 \\]\n\\[ 12 - 5 = 7k \\]\n\\[ 7 = 7k \\]\n\\[ k = 1 \\]\nHere, \\(k = 1\\) is an integer. So, \\(n = 4\\) and \\(k = 1\\) is a particular solution to the equation \\(3n - 7k = 5\\).\n\nThe general solution to the equation \\(3n - 7k = 5\\) can be written as:\n\\[ n = 4 + 7t \\]\n\\[ k = 1 + 3t \\]\nfor any integer \\(t\\).\n\nWe need to find the largest value of \\(a_n = 2 + 3n\\) that is less than 500. Substituting \\(n = 4 + 7t\\) into the expression for \\(a_n\\), we get:\n\\[ a_n = 2 + 3(4 + 7t) = 2 + 12 + 21t = 14 + 21t \\]\nWe want \\(14 + 21t < 500\\):\n\\[ 14 + 21t < 500 \\]\n\\[ 21t < 486 \\]\n\\[ t < \\frac{486}{21} \\]\n\\[ t < 23.0476 \\]\nThe largest integer \\(t\\) is 23. Substituting \\(t = 23\\) into the expression for \\(a_n\\), we get:\n\\[ a_n = 14 + 21(23) = 14 + 483 = 497 \\]\nTherefore, the largest value less than 500 that the two arithmetic progressions have in common is:\n\\[ \\boxed{497} \\]",
